From 48070c3945f5837d196dae17e0317ebed5bdafd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gdr-arch22 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:34:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [chunk_gated_delta_rule] arch22: cut the Stage2 serial
 chain, hand-written cube pipeline, 2D partition and cross-sequence packing

Five independent changes to the arch22 (910B/v220) kernel. Every one is bit-exact
against the stock operator: 36/36 output tensors compare equal with torch.equal
across 18 shapes, covering odd/even chunk counts, sequences spanning chunk groups,
varlen batches, the unaligned-tail and non-packable fallbacks, g=None, zero and
random initial states, and TP1 head counts.

1. Stage2 state ping-pong
   curState and finalState aliased one GM buffer, so the AIV had to wait for the
   AIC to finish reading the old state (CrossCore flag 0x2) before writing the
   scaled state back. Read and write buffers are now separated by chunk parity,
   reusing the never-read highState_ workspace as the second buffer (its fp32 size
   is twice the bf16 state, so no workspace growth). The 0x2 edge disappears and
   the AIV's 32KB read-scale-write overlaps the AIC's two matmuls. Phase is
   anchored at the group tail so the last chunk always lands in finalState.

2. Hand-written basic-API matmul (chunk_gated_delta_rule_matmul_basic.h)
   Replaces all ten MatmulImpl call sites (six in stage1, three in stage2, one in
   stage3) with an explicit GM->L1(Nd2Nz) -> L1->L0(LoadData/LoadDataWithTranspose)
   -> Mmad -> Fixpipe(V220) pipeline. Every matmul in this operator fits a single
   128x128 tile, so each Execute is single-shot. This ports the arch35
   CGDRMatmulBasic approach to v220 APIs, keeping its BSource::SameAsA reuse for
   stage1's K@K^T.

3. Two-deep software pipelining across matmul calls
   L1/L0A/L0B/L0C are double-buffered by call parity, using L0A/L0B/L0C in full.
   Call i's top gate waits on call i-2's Fixpipe of the same buffer set, which
   transitively protects every on-chip buffer. The two call pairs with a GM
   read-after-write dependency are marked serial. stage2's CalAttnInter reuses the
   state operand already resident in the other set's L1B.

4. Stage2 (Nv, Dv) 2D partition
   Stage2 partitioned on Nv alone, so with Nv=4 only 4 of 20 cube cores worked.
   Dv is now split in two when twice the unit count still fits the core count, and
   only when Dv is even. Both gates fall back to the original single-unit path, so
   shapes that do not benefit run the original code.

5. Cross-sequence chunk-group packing
   Each batch entry ran as its own serial group of stage1/stage2/stage3 plus three
   SyncAll. When every sequence but the last is chunkSize-aligned, chunk groups are
   now packed across sequences and stage2 walks (bid, nv[, Dv slice]) segment
   chains, carrying state across groups through finalState. The last sequence may
   have any length: its tail chunk is the batch's tail chunk, which stage1 and
   stage3 already handle. Any other alignment falls back to the per-sequence path.

Operator-level, 910B4-1, CANN 9.1.0, paired per-process runs, median of 30,
at Qwen3.6-35B-A3B GDN shapes (Dk=Dv=128, global Nk=16/Nv=32):

  TP8  (Nk=2, Nv=4)   T=8192 B=1     1461.5 -> 1186.7 us   -18.8%
  TP8                 T=8192 B=16    2152.8 ->  989.4 us   -54.0%
  TP8                 T=2560 B=40    2537.5 ->  372.4 us   -85.3%
  TP4  (Nk=4, Nv=8)   T=8192 B=1     1995.1 -> 1673.7 us   -16.1%
  TP1  (Nk=16,Nv=32)  T=8192 B=1     4893.3 -> 4310.9 us   -11.9%

End-to-end on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-w8a8 (TP4, vllm bench serve, 4-arm ABBA with two
consecutive passes per server instance, all eight medians non-overlapping;
random 1024-token prompts, 128 requests, concurrency 32):

  Median TTFT   1462.4 -> 1392.6 ms   -4.8%
  Mean TTFT     1408.9 -> 1346.0 ms   -4.5%
  Request tput   20.62 ->  21.60 r/s  +4.8%

The end-to-end figure is bounded by the operator's share of prefill (~16% for this
model), and it grows with how many sequences prefill in the same step, which is
what changes 4 and 5 address.

Ideas 1-3 apply to arch35 as well; the structural items behind 4 and 5 are present
there verbatim (Process(), nvPerCore, the subBlockIdx==1 return).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
